# Harklight for self-hosted agents

Use this skill with a self-hosted Harklight Server. Ask its operator for the
server address, certificate hostname, public CA certificate, person account,
existing enrolled device directory and allowed sender ID. Verify the CA through
the operator's trusted channel; network reachability alone establishes no
account trust. Resolve missing configuration with that operator rather than
copying private keys, inventing endpoint ports or changing grants to make a
send succeed. Installing this skill grants no credentials or messaging authority.

Agent conversations use the separately released `harklight-agent` CLI described
below. The server package does not bundle that executable. For enrollment and
registered-agent tools, set `PACKAGE` to the extracted package directory and
`HARKLIGHT_SERVER_CLI` to its `bin/harklight`; these tools do not start a second server. The package's
`SELF_HOST.md` explains server setup and port exposure. A self-hosted origin may
publish `none` as its file upload cap; use its published limits.

## Explicitly authorized enrollment

Use an existing enrollment for ordinary sends. If asked to enroll a new device,
the server package's headless CLI creates its identity locally. The person owner
approves the displayed code and public-key fingerprint for the intended person
and label. The owner can grant a sender pattern during approval with `--senders`
or later with `owner grant-sender --device FINGERPRINT --sender PATTERN`.
Omit sender grants for a read/respond-only device. Approval does not make the
device an owner. Sender-pattern matching is defined in the shared core below.

For example, on the agent's machine, with values supplied by the operator:

```sh
"$PACKAGE/bin/harklight" enroll start --directory "$DEVICE_DIR" \
  --connect "$SERVER_ADDRESS" --name "$SERVER_NAME" --port "$ENROLL_PORT" \
  --ca "$CA_PEM" --label "Script sender"
```

Give the approval code to the authenticated owner. The owner checks the
intended person, label and key fingerprint before running `bin/harklight owner
approve` with `--senders app:example` for this sender. For an explicitly
authorized enrollment, check the saved request with the installed enrollment
command. This does not prove session listener support:

```sh
"$PACKAGE/bin/harklight" enroll status --directory "$DEVICE_DIR" \
  --connect "$SERVER_ADDRESS" --name "$SERVER_NAME" --port "$ENROLL_PORT" \
  --relay-port "$RELAY_PORT" --ca "$CA_PEM"
```

Keep the directory if a submission reply is lost. `enroll retry` uses the same
local key; a `key_pending` result does not recover the old code or prove
enrollment. Follow the command's expiry guidance, and treat `key_in_use` or an
unknown status handle as unresolved until the owner reconciles it. This
headless enrollment procedure is distinct from the native app's guided device
joining.

The server's earlier device interface remains available for existing device
workflows: `device read` journals replayed inbox entries before ACK;
`device respond --sender ID --message-id ID --answer answer.json` deliberately
submits one JSON answer. `device receipt --sender ID --message-id ID` is available
only to the original sending device and stays pending until a terminal exists.
Supply the same device directory, trusted CA and relay endpoint as enrollment
status. These are distinct from the origin conversation feed and registered-agent
search below; use the session CLI for new agent messages. Preserve the exact
identity, request and journal for uncertain device operations; identical retries
reuse their saved operation. A different payload under an accepted message ID
conflicts rather than revising it.
